  "name": "locale-prefs-disabled-locale-no-wipe-modern-debug",
  "description": "Issue #18 sprint 4: a real ExpansionUserPrefs record whose stored localeId (qps-ploc, 7) IS a supported slot but is not enabled by THIS build's own compiled-in mask is classified EXPANSION_USER_PREFS_DISABLED_LOCALE by the real ExpansionUserPrefs_ValidateRaw()/Normalize() pair, requiring a re-prompt (here AUTO_SELECT collapses it, since the default build only enables one locale). Proves the re-prompt/auto-correct path never wipes SRAM: both checkpoints hash the WHOLE 0x8000 image excluding (a) the 12-byte ExpansionUserPrefs sub-record itself (offset 0x73D4 -- the only bytes this path is allowed to rewrite), (b) the vanilla SoundRoomSaveData struct (include/bmsave.h, offset 0x7224, size 0x24), and (c) the vanilla SramInit() hardware self-test scratch pad (gSram->reserved, include/bmsave.h, offset 0x73A0, size 4) -- three pre-existing, locale-unrelated regions that ordinary vanilla boot-time bookkeeping legitimately rewrites on every boot regardless of prefs state, confirmed via a full-0x8000-byte chunked diff sweep during this sprint (see reports/issue18_localization_closure.md). Rather than widening the hash-exclusion blind spot further, this scenario now also independently probes every excluded/adjacent protected region byte-by-byte (GBA SRAM is 8-bit-wide hardware -- multi-byte reads from it alias a single byte across all lanes, so each field below is probed one byte at a time) at both the pre-runtimeinit baseline and the post-decision-settled checkpoint: the SramInit() self-test pad itself, ExpansionSaveMeta's magic/checksum, and the not-yet-populated XMAP save header's magic/checksum/save_magic32 -- proving those regions are stable/known rather than silently masked, without hand-writing any of their values. An equal sram_hash at both checkpoints -- captured for real, not hand-written -- is empirical proof that InitGlobalSaveInfodata()'s full-SRAM wipe path was never taken.",
